course: world
lesson: 83
title: Floating and sinking: the push of water
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When you put something in water, the water pushes it upwards. This push is called upthrust. You can feel it when you try to push a beach ball under the water and it pushes back up.
explain: An object in water pushes some water out of the way; we say it displaces the water. The upthrust is equal to the weight of the water pushed aside. This was discovered over 2,000 years ago by the Greek thinker Archimedes.
explain: If the upthrust can grow as big as the object's weight, the object floats; if the object sinks fully under and the upthrust is still smaller than its weight, it sinks. A lump of clay sinks, but shaped into a hollow boat it pushes aside much more water and floats.
explain: Salty water is heavier than the same amount of fresh water, so it pushes up harder. That is why people float easily in the very salty Dead Sea, and why a ship sits a little higher in the sea than in a river.
explain: A submarine can choose to float or sink. It lets water into its tanks to become heavier and dive, and blows the water out with air to become lighter and rise.
fact: Water pushes up on anything placed in it; this push is called upthrust.
fact: A floating object pushes aside, or displaces, some water.
fact: The upthrust equals the weight of the water that the object pushes aside.
fact: An object floats when the upthrust is equal to its weight.
fact: A hollow shape pushes aside more water than a solid lump of the same material.
fact: The more cargo a ship carries, the lower it sits in the water.
fact: Salt water gives more upthrust than fresh water.
fact: A submarine dives by filling its tanks with water and rises by blowing them out with air.
fact: Many fish have a swim bladder, a bag of gas that helps them float at the depth they want.
fact: Ice floats because water expands when it freezes, so ice is lighter than the same amount of water.
fact: Oil floats on water because it is less dense than water: a cupful of oil weighs less than a cupful of water.
fact: Things feel lighter in water because the upthrust holds up part of their weight.
exercise: What do we call the upward push of water on an object? | Upthrust | Gravity | Friction | Pressure from the air
exercise: Why is it hard to push a beach ball under water? | The water pushes up on it strongly | The air inside the ball is very heavy | The ball is pulled up by the sky | The ball's skin is too slippery
exercise: Who first explained that the upthrust equals the weight of the water pushed aside? | Archimedes | Isaac Newton | Galileo | Thomas Edison
exercise: Why does a lump of clay sink but the same clay shaped like a boat float? | The boat shape pushes aside much more water | The boat shape makes the clay much lighter than before | The boat shape traps air that pulls it up | The boat shape changes the clay into wood
exercise: A ship is loaded with heavy boxes. What happens? | It sits lower in the water | It sits higher in the water | It stays at exactly the same level | It sinks as soon as anything is added
exercise: Why is it easier to float in the sea than in a swimming pool? | Salt water pushes up harder than fresh water | Sea waves lift you up all the time | The sea is deeper, so it holds you up better than a pool | Sea water is warmer, so you float better
exercise: How does a submarine dive? | It lets water into its tanks to become heavier | It pumps air into its tanks to become heavier | It turns its engine off and drops down | It folds its sides to become smaller
exercise: How does a submarine come back up to the surface? | It blows the water out of its tanks with air | It lets more water into its tanks to get lighter | It throws its heavy anchor away to the bottom | It spins its propeller round the other way to rise
exercise: What helps many fish float at the depth they choose? | A swim bladder full of gas | Their fins filled with fat | Their light hollow scales | Holding their breath like we do
exercise: Why do icebergs float? | Ice is less dense than liquid water | Ice is full of air from the wind | Icebergs are held up by the cold | Salt in the sea freezes underneath them
exercise: You pour oil into a glass of water. Where does the oil go? | It floats on top of the water | It sinks to the bottom of the glass | It mixes evenly with the water | It hangs in the middle of the glass
exercise: Why does a heavy stone feel lighter when you lift it under water? | The water's upthrust holds up part of its weight | The stone loses some of its mass while it is in water | Water makes gravity weaker for a moment | Your arms grow stronger under water
exercise: Why does a life jacket help a person float? | It is big but light, so it pushes aside a lot of water | It is very heavy and holds you steady in the water | It makes the water around you saltier and thicker | It warms the water around you so it pushes harder
exercise: A huge steel ship floats but a small steel nail sinks. Why? | The ship's hollow shape displaces a lot of water | Big things always float and small things sink | The ship's engines keep it on the surface | Steel floats in the sea but not in a glass of tap water
test: An egg sinks in a glass of tap water. You stir in lots of salt and the egg rises. Why? | The salty water pushes up harder on the egg | The salt makes the egg lighter inside | The salt sticks to the egg and lifts it | The stirring pushes the egg up for good and it stays there
test: Which is true about a floating object? | The water it pushes aside weighs the same as it does | It pushes aside no water at all | It has no weight while it floats | It floats only because it contains air, which has no weight
test: A sailor watches a cargo ship as its boxes are taken off in port. What does the sailor see? | The ship slowly rises higher in the water | The ship slowly sinks lower in the water | The ship stays exactly where it was in the water | The ship tips over onto one side
test: You squeeze a ball of foil tightly into a tiny lump. It sinks, though a loose foil boat floats. Why? | The tight lump pushes aside too little water | Squeezing it makes the foil much heavier than before | Squeezing turns the foil into a new and heavier metal | The boat floats only because its inside stays dry
test: A boat sails from a river into the sea. What happens? | It floats a little higher in the salty sea | It floats a little lower in the salty sea | It floats at exactly the same height | It must take on water to stay balanced
test: What would happen to a pond in winter if ice were denser than water? | Ice would sink and the pond could freeze from the bottom up | Ice would float even higher than it does now, right on top of the pond | The pond could never freeze at all | The ice would turn back into water at once
test: A diver wants to stay still in the middle of the water. What should be true? | The upthrust must equal the diver's weight | The upthrust must be much bigger than the weight | The diver must breathe out all the air | The diver must weigh nothing in the water
test: Which object is most likely to float? | A big hollow plastic box with its lid shut | A small solid lump of iron | A glass marble | A handful of coins
test: Why would a submarine with empty tanks find it hard to stay under water? | It would be too light, so the upthrust pushes it up | It would be too heavy and would fall too fast | Its engine needs water in the tanks to work at all, so it stops | Empty tanks stop the propeller from turning
test: how does a massive steel ship float when a nail sinks | Its hollow shape pushes aside lots of water, giving enough upthrust | Steel floats once it is very big | The engines push it up out of the water | Air from the sea holds the ship up
test: why does the boat sit lower when u load it up | More weight means it must push aside more water to float | The water gets thinner under heavy boats, so they sink lower | Heavy boats leak and fill with water | The cargo pulls the water down with it
test: Ava says ice floats cos it's got air bubbles in it. true? | No, water expands when it freezes, so ice is lighter than the same amount of water | Yes, only the bubbles keep ice up | Yes, ice without bubbles always sinks | No, ice floats because it is colder than the water
test: my brother reckons it's easier to float in a pool than in the sea. right? | No, salt water gives more upthrust than fresh water | Yes, fresh water pushes up harder than salt water | Yes, but only if the pool is heated | No, both give exactly the same upthrust
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises upthrust? | The push of water upwards upon an object placed in it | The pull of the Earth downwards upon an object | The friction between a boat and the water | The weight of an object measured in air
exam: It is given that cargo is loaded upon a ship. Determine the consequent alteration in its position in the water. | It sits lower in the water | It sits higher in the water | It remains at exactly the same level | It rises entirely out of the water
exam: Identify the principal reason for which ice floats upon water. | Water expands when frozen, so ice is lighter than the same amount of water | Ice contains bubbles of a gas lighter than air | Ice is pushed upwards by the cold of the water | Ice possesses no weight whatsoever
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the upthrust of salt water and fresh water. | Salt water gives more upthrust than fresh water | Fresh water gives more upthrust than salt water | Both give exactly the same upthrust | Salt water gives no upthrust whatsoever
